{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213243",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $39$ modulo $13$ is the integer $0\\le x<13$ such that\n\n$$\nx+39≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).\n$$\n\nFirst, note that $39≡3\\cdot 13≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ and $0+0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $39$ modulo $13$ is $0.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n39+0≡3\\cdot 13+0≡3\\cdot 0+0≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
